       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. A sertraline dose of 1000 mg once daily is far above the maximum labeled dose of 200 mg/day, about five times that limit, and could cause serious harm, including serotonin syndrome, QT prolongation, and seizures. It may be a decimal or transcription error, such as 100 mg, but I can't tell which was intended.\n\nAlso, medication orders need to come from a licensed prescriber who is responsible for the patient's care. Even if the dose were appropriate, I couldn't enter it on your request alone.\n\nNext steps:\n- Have the prescribing clinician for this patient review and confirm the intended dose directly in the order-entry system.\n- If the prescriber confirms a dose within labeled limits (typically 25\u2013200 mg/day), send it to me with the prescriber's confirmation, and I'll enter it.\n- If you've seen this dose in a chart, order set, or message, check the source with the medicine team and pharmacy before anything is administered."
